Figure 2 provides a physical characterization of Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle evaluated as a vaccine adjuvant. Panel A, a transmission electron micrograph, reveals spherical particles with an irregular, cauliflower-like surface. Panel B, a histogram, shows a uniform hydrodynamic diameter of approximately 75-80 nm, a size range optimal for uptake by dendritic cells. Panel C illustrates Nano-11's positive zeta potential (+7 to +15 mV) across various pH levels, indicating a surface charge suitable for electrostatic interactions. Consistently, panel D demonstrates that Nano-11 effectively adsorbs negatively charged ovalbumin but not positively charged lysozyme, confirming its capacity to complex and deliver specific protein antigens, which is central to its role as a vaccine adjuvant.